@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for macOS (Apple silicon and Intel)
=======================================================================

@TITLE@ (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run natively: the game's own
program translated into C++ and compiled for your Mac, drawn with Vulkan
(through MoltenVK, which comes with the app).

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
its disc image extracted to a folder (for example with extract-xiso or
xdvdfs). That gives a folder holding default.xbe and the folders bundles,
demand, xwavebank and the rest.

This is a work in progress: the game plays, and some things are still
missing or wrong. Changes: CHANGELOG.md on the project's page.


Playing
-------
1. Drag @TITLE@.app onto Applications (in this disk image's window).
   Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open @TITLE@ from Applications. The first time, macOS stops it
   (the app isn't signed by an Apple developer account, see below): open
   System Settings, Privacy & Security, scroll down to "@TITLE@ was
   blocked", click Open Anyway and confirm with your password. macOS asks
   only once.
3. The game is compiled with Apple's own compiler, free from Apple (its
   licence doesn't let it come with this download). If your Mac doesn't have
   it yet, the app says so: click "Install Apple's command line tools",
   follow Apple's installer (a few minutes), then click "Check again".
4. Click "Choose your disc folder" and pick the folder you extracted the
   disc into (the one holding default.xbe), or drag the folder onto the
   window. It tells you straight away if it's the wrong game or another
   release.
5. Wait for the first build: a few minutes. Your game files never leave your
   Mac.
6. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs.

After that, the app remembers your disc folder, and Play starts the game
straight away. After an update it builds the game again first, recompiling
only what changed.

The game's output is in run.log and run-errors.log, and the build's in
build.log, all in ~/Library/Application Support/qilin/@GAME@/work (in
Finder: Go, Go to Folder). Send them when reporting a problem. If the
picture is wrong, press F9 while it shows: the next 12 frames and what the
game drew in them are saved to a file in the data folder's captures folder
(the game says where). Send that file too.

About "Open Anyway": the app is signed only by itself ("ad hoc"), not by an
Apple developer account, so Gatekeeper can't name who made it. Check that you
downloaded it from the project's GitHub releases page (below) before you
open it. Nothing in it changes that check or works around it.

Needs: macOS 14.5 (Sonoma) or newer, on an Apple silicon or Intel Mac, and
Apple's command line tools from Xcode 16 or newer, which need that macOS
(the app checks; older tools are updated with System Settings, General,
Software Update). Graphics go through MoltenVK, Vulkan over Apple's Metal.


Controls
--------
Any controller macOS or SDL knows (Xbox, PlayStation, Switch, MFi), as the
Xbox controller, or the keyboard as controller 1:

  Xbox controller      Keyboard
  left stick           W A S D
  right stick          numeric keypad 8 4 5 6
  D-pad                arrows
  A B X Y              Space or K, L, J, I
  White, Black         Q, E   (left and right shoulder buttons)
  triggers             Left Shift, Right Shift or O
  Start, Back          Return, Delete or Tab
  stick clicks         F, H

F1 or Esc, or Back and Start together on a controller: the settings menu
(the game pauses): the display, the controls and the game's sound and
language. On a Mac keyboard F1 may need the fn key. Option+Return, F11, or
the window's green button: full screen.

Saves and settings are kept in ~/Library/Application
Support/qilin/@GAME@ (the settings in ghoulies.ini), with the disc folder
you chose, the build (work) and its cache of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in the app
-----------------
Right-click @TITLE@.app, Show Package Contents:

Contents/MacOS/Play @TITLE@
                                  chooses your disc folder, builds the game
                                  (once) and starts it
Contents/Resources/kit/qilin      the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@
                                  into C++
Contents/Resources/kit/lib, include
                                  the prebuilt rest of the game: its
                                  runtime, the Xbox's kernel, Direct3D and
                                  DirectSound replaced natively, the
                                  renderer, the window, controls and sound
                                  (SDL3), the settings menu (Dear ImGui)
Contents/Resources/kit/files.txt  every file this release holds (see below)
Contents/Frameworks/libMoltenVK.dylib
                                  MoltenVK: Vulkan over Metal (Khronos)
Contents/Resources/LICENSES       the licences of everything included

The game itself (@EXE@), built from your disc, is in the work folder above,
not in the app: the app is never changed by building.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/@REPO@


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in the app asks GitHub for a newer release (it sends
nothing about you or your Mac). If there is one, it downloads the disk
image, copies the new app beside this one and swaps it in, then starts
again and builds the game for the new version. If anything fails on the
way, the old version is left as it was. The app must be somewhere you can
write to for that (Applications is, for an administrator): run from the
disk image itself, it can't update. You can also drag a new download's app
over this one in Applications: it finds your disc folder and the cache by
itself. Your saves and settings are never in the app, so nothing of yours
is touched.

The game is compiled for this Mac's processor; on another Mac, it is built
again. Compiled files come from the cache when nothing they're made from has
changed. A failed build leaves the working game as it was; the one before a
new build is kept as work/@EXE@.previous until the new one has run and
closed normally. "Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.

Before changing an existing title-save file, the runtime keeps five copies
under the data folder: save-history/partition1/<relative file>/1.bin
through 5.bin (1 is the newest). If a copy can't be made, the write is
refused. To restore one: quit the game, keep a separate copy of the current
partition1, and copy the chosen snapshot back to partition1/<relative
file>.
